  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1: Dry and cool conditions continue through the \nearly week, then temperatures gradually warm to seasonable \nvalues by Wednesday.\n\nAs a widespread freeze occurred across the region yesterday \nmorning, the National Weather Service Charleston has \ndiscontinued the Frost Advisories and Freeze Watches/Warnings \nuntil March 1st, 2027.\n\nHigh pressure will continue to slowly expand into the region \nover the next 24 hours, yielding another night of ideal \nradiational cooling conditions. Expect another round of cold \novernight temperatures as the forecast notes low 30s across the \ninland counties and upper 30s to low 40s along the coastal \ncorridor. Taking a look at latest observations and accounting \nfor what happened yesterday morning, we've nudged the overnight \ntemperatures down 1-2 degrees.\n\nAs we enter daybreak, temperatures will slowly warm-up with \nafternoon highs in the low to mid 60s across the region combined\nwith partly to mostly cloudy skies. Northeasterly winds will \nslowly tick upwards through the evening with speeds 10 to 15 mph\nwith gusts up to 20 mph (highest along the coastline). \nConditions will remain rain-free under this synoptic pattern. \nExpect Monday night/Tuesday morning to be a tad warmer with \novernight lows in the mid to upper 30s across the inland and \nlow to mid 40s along the coastal corridor.\n\nThrough Mid-Week: Surface high pressure will remain centered \nover the Mid-Atlantic through mid-week, yielding a dry forecast \nand warming temperatures. High temperatures on Tuesday will \nreach into the mid to upper 60s, warming to the low 70s on \nWednesday. Conditions overnight will not be as conducive to \nradiational cooling as was observed this last weekend with some \nhigh clouds and winds remaining around 5-10 mph overnight. Lows \non Tuesday night will bottom out in the upper 30s far inland \nwith near 50 along the coast. Slightly warmer lows are in the \nforecast for Wednesday night, with mid 40s inland and mid 50s \nalong the coast.",
